1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,340
Every company's data lives in hundreds
of tables wired together by pipelines

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:05,780
nobody fully remembers.

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:09,620
DataHub is an open source
catalog that keeps that map.

4
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:12,340
Every table, every column, every pipeline.

5
00:00:12,765 --> 00:00:15,265
Here is the failure it
cannot fix on its own.

6
00:00:15,405 --> 00:00:17,585
Someone upstream renames one column.

7
00:00:17,725 --> 00:00:18,625
Nothing crashes.

8
00:00:18,685 --> 00:00:22,925
The pipelines below keep running, quietly
producing wrong numbers, and nobody

9
00:00:22,925 --> 00:00:24,145
notices for days.

10
00:00:24,525 --> 00:00:26,225
So let's break it on purpose.

11
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:28,200
One change to the live catalog.

12
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:32,360
In the orders table, order placed
at becomes order created at.

13
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:36,120
Now the catalog shows drift
and nobody downstream was told.

14
00:00:36,340 --> 00:00:37,880
Here is the detector's evidence.

15
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:40,695
On the left, the columns
DataHub had on record.

16
00:00:40,775 --> 00:00:43,115
On the right, what is
in the warehouse now.

17
00:00:43,255 --> 00:00:45,275
Every row matches but one.

18
00:00:45,575 --> 00:00:47,275
Now the agent goes to work.

19
00:00:47,335 --> 00:00:49,115
Watch the timeline on the right.

20
00:00:49,175 --> 00:00:54,650
Those violet chips are its live questions
to DataHub asked through MCP, the

21
00:00:54,650 --> 00:00:57,550
connector that lets an agent
query a catalog directly.

22
00:00:58,090 --> 00:01:02,350
Search the catalog, list this table's
columns, trace this column downstream.

23
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:06,270
Every answer comes from the
catalog, not a text search.

24
00:01:06,585 --> 00:01:09,405
Here is the result, and it is
the heart of this project.

25
00:01:09,625 --> 00:01:14,365
Column level lineage maps exactly which
downstream code reads this one column,

26
00:01:14,425 --> 00:01:16,125
not merely which tables connect.

27
00:01:16,425 --> 00:01:18,765
So you get three answers, not two.

28
00:01:19,110 --> 00:01:23,930
Three files in amber genuinely read
the changed column and need fixing.

29
00:01:24,390 --> 00:01:29,450
Two in blue read a renamed copy made
upstream, so they need nothing.

30
00:01:29,910 --> 00:01:32,650
Seven in gray are correctly left alone.

31
00:01:33,015 --> 00:01:35,435
Click one of the seven,
and it shows its work.

32
00:01:35,495 --> 00:01:40,235
It really does sit downstream, but the
columns it reads are order ID, quantity,

33
00:01:40,295 --> 00:01:41,435
and unit price.

34
00:01:41,575 --> 00:01:43,275
None of them is the one that changed.

35
00:01:43,415 --> 00:01:45,195
Anyone can list everything downstream.

36
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:48,220
Knowing what genuinely
breaks is the hard part.

37
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:49,660
The fixes are surgical.

38
00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:52,380
Here's the same file before and after.

39
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:54,860
Only the tokens that changed have moved.

40
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,380
The rewrite is done by SQL Glot, a parser
that edits the syntax tree directly.

41
00:02:00,795 --> 00:02:02,555
The model never writes code.

42
00:02:02,555 --> 00:02:07,275
It writes the explanation, and nothing
ships until every column reference checks

43
00:02:07,275 --> 00:02:07,875
out.

44
00:02:07,995 --> 00:02:13,455
23 of 23 resolved, and it shows where each
answer came from, live DataHub schemas,

45
00:02:13,515 --> 00:02:15,935
the repaired schema, or the query itself.

46
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:19,120
One unresolved reference
blocks the whole patch.

47
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:23,420
Then it opens a real pull request, a
proposed code change sent to a person

48
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:27,820
to review, carrying the lineage path
from the changed column to every file

49
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:28,480
it touched.

50
00:02:28,755 --> 00:02:33,495
Finally, it writes the repair back into
DataHub so the catalog is true again.

51
00:02:33,795 --> 00:02:38,535
Lineage corrected, the column documented,
and an incident logged for sign off.

52
00:02:38,755 --> 00:02:41,974
The next engineer or the
next agent inherits it.

53
00:02:42,134 --> 00:02:45,114
Column level lineage to
pinpoint what genuinely broke.

54
00:02:45,334 --> 00:02:47,754
Syntax tree edits so the fix is exact.

55
00:02:47,974 --> 00:02:50,954
And the catalog as the source
of truth at both ends.
